</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:120%;">D.4 Sampling Temperature</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also evaluated Drax under different sampling temperatures on the Multilingual LibriSpeech (MLS) benchmark. As expected, increasing temperature leads to more diverse generations but also higher error rates. Lower temperatures (e.g., 0.01-0.1) yield the best WER across languages, while higher values such as 1.0 noticeably degrade performance. Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ D.4 Sampling Temperature ‣ Appendix D Additional Results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reports total WERs for each language at different temperatures.</p>
</div>
