</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:120%;">D.5 Effect of NFE</h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number of NFE is a key hyperparameter for NAR based generative models. Here we show that the Drax model achieves high quality sampling with as few as 4-16 sampling steps. Figure <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ D.2 Drax-flash Results ‣ Appendix D Additional Results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show the per-language WER for the MLS and VoxPupoli dataset as a function of NFE. The plot shows that Drax WER drops quickly, with relevantly small improvement for <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{NFE}\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>NFE</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{NFE}\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:120%;">D.6 Sampling with and without <math id="A4.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in the Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the intermediate distribution <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is introduced as an auxiliary component during training in order to better align the training and inference occupancies. Generally, we do not use <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math>
during inference. To verify this design choice, we compare generation with and
without <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math> at sampling time (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Tri-mixture Velocities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). The results, reported in Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title='Figure 9 ‣ D.6 Sampling with and without 𝑝_"mid" ‣ Appendix D Additional Results' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, show that including <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math> during generation consistently hurts performance across datasets. This supports our design choice to treat <math id="A4.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a training-only component.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04162v1/figures/p_mid_wer_bar.png" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/221;" width="333" height="221" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A4.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of using <math id="A4.F9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math> during sampling.
Results show that including <math id="A4.F9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math> at inference degrades accuracy,
confirming our choice to use it only during training.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:120%;">D.7 Samples from Audio Distribution</h3>

<div id="A4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ D.8 Generation Path ‣ Appendix D Additional Results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows an example of samples from the learned middle distribution <math id="A4.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can see that some samples are acoustically plausible but imperfect.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:120%;">D.8 Generation Path</h3>

<div id="A4.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the decoding dynamics, Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ D.8 Generation Path ‣ Appendix D Additional Results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> traces Drax’s stepwise refinement on a single example. Each row corresponds to a generation step and each column to a token position; cells show the token committed at that step, while “_” denotes positions that remain unchanged from the initial noisy state. The table shows how tokens progressively stabilize across steps, first in shorter blocks and then as longer spans, until the full sequence is recovered.</p>
</div>
